.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Huh
and
.I Bits
color the text of the
.IR acme (1)
window
.B $winid
by what a language model makes of it: how many bits each token costs, from
.IR lmfs ,
or
.IR lmsim (4)
in tests, mounted on
.I lmdir
.RB ( /mnt/lm ).
If
.I lmdir
has no
.BR clone ,
it mounts
.B /srv/lm
there, as
.B lmsim
.B -s
.B lm
posts it, since acme runs a command in acme's own namespace.
Each is meant to be run from the window's tag.
One of them follows a window at a time, as the window's
.B event
has one owner: a second program on a window, under
.IR amux (4),
cannot open it and exits, and one that starts on a window whose
.I astyles
layers already include its own deletes its layer and exits.
To change one for the other, turn the first off by its name in the tag, then start the other.
.I Huh
highlights the stretches where the text's information is, those that cost the most;
.I Bits
colors every segment of the text in one of seven bands of bits a rune, and draws the text as a strip in a
window of its own.
.PP
Each writes a layer of
.IR astyles (4)
on the window,
.B huh
or
.BR bits .
It makes the layer, opens the window's
.B event
file, and then reads its body, so that no edit falls between.
It writes the text to a session of
.IR lmdir 's
.B data
file, after a preamble, the window's name as its tag has it, a colon and a blank line, as
.IR bits (1)
writes a file's, and leaves out the answer's lines for the preamble; a window with no name gets
none.
It marks the tokens it gets back and writes the layer;
.I Bits
splits them into segments first, as
.IR segment (1)
does.
Then it follows the window's edits, by the
.B I
and
.B D
events, in a copy of the body: after each one it writes the whole copy to
.B data
on a new open and closes the old one, which drops what was left of it, marks again, and writes the layer again, under
.IR astyles 's
.B seq
guard, so a layer from an older text is not kept.
.PP
.I Huh
highlights, in the color
.BR huh ,
the stretches that
.I huh
prints, below, with
.IR bits ,
.I width
and
.I tokens
as its
.BR -s ,
.B -w
and
.BR -l ,
and segments nothing.
The cutoffs move only when the text has been idle for a second and the whole answer has come;
until then the tokens an edit changed are judged against the cutoffs as they stand, so the
highlights near an edit change at once and the rest stay as they were.
While a text is first scored, the cutoffs come from the tokens scored so far.
.PP
The density is the share of the text that
.I Huh
highlights, in percent, 10 by default, from 5 to 30.
.B /lib/huh
sets the default and
.B $home/lib/huh
the user's, by a line
.BI density " n"\f1,
the second overriding the first.
.I Huh+
and
.IR Huh- ,
links to
.IR Huh ,
raise and lower it by 5 and write
.BR $home/lib/huh ,
as
.I Huh
with a
.I density
does, swept in the tag as
.BR "Huh 15" ;
they need no window.
A running
.I Huh
reads the files again when they change, and marks again by the new density at once.
.PP
The colors are tints the programs give in their layers' own color lines, each with an opacity,
so that
.IR astyles (4)
blends them over the text's other colors in either theme.
.IR Bits '
.I penalty
and
.I runes
are
.IR segment 's
.B -p
and
.BR -l .
.PP
.I Bits
colors each segment in its color,
.B bits0
to
.BR bits6 ,
by its bits a rune: under 0.5, 0.5 to 1, 1 to 2, 2 to 3, 3 to 4.5, 4.5 to 6, and 6 or more.
It also opens a drawn window, named for the file with
.BR +Bits ,
that shows the text as a strip, a row for each of its lines and a block for each word, in its
band's color, and beside it a bar for each paragraph, from
.B bits
.BR -p :
its length the paragraph's bits alone, and its dark part those the text before it already had.
Button 1 on the strip selects its token in the text's window and shows it;
button 1 on a bar, its paragraph's start.
Button 3 shows the token's alternatives, as below.
.PP
The program reaches the window's files through
.IR acme (2).
It takes the window's events by reading the window's
.B event
file, whoever else reads it.
Of the window's events:
.TF Bits
.TP
.B Huh
(in
.IR Huh 's
window) or
.B Bits
(in
.IR Bits 's)
ends the program, so the same word in the tag turns it on and off.
.TP
.B Get
is written back; the program reads the reloaded body and colors it again.
.TP
.B Del
is written back, and the program ends.
.TP
Button 3
clicked, not swept, on a mark (in
.IR Huh )
or on colored text (in
.IR Bits ),
writes to the window's
.B +Errors
a line for the token clicked and one for each of the run's three costliest tokens:
the token's address in the file, the token, its bits and rank, and the model's five likeliest
tokens in its place, from
.IR lmdir 's
.BR top ,
each with its bits.
B3 on a line shows its token.
The alternatives tell a misspelling, whose likeliest token is the right spelling, from a new
idea.
.PD
.LP
Every other event goes back to acme, which handles it as it would have.
However the program ends, by an event, a failure of
.IR lmdir ,
or a note, it deletes its layer and its own window, and closes the event files.
Del or
.B Bits
in
.IR Bits 's
own window ends it too.
.PP
.B Huh
.B -a
writes the alternatives for the token at the window's dot to
.B +Errors
and exits, in any window, with or without
.I Huh
or
.IR Bits ;
.B cmd-k
.B h
in
.B /lib/hotkey/acme
runs it on the window with the focus.
.PP
The command
.I huh
scores each
.IR file ,
or its standard input, once, and prints a line for each highlighted stretch:
.IP
.EX
README.md:#2210,#2398 3.41
.EE
.LP
its address and its score.
A file goes to the model after its preamble, as in
.IR bits (1),
and its addresses are
.IR file :# q0 ,# q1 .
Standard input has no name, so it gets no preamble and its addresses are
.RI # q0 ,# q1
alone;
.B -n
gives it
.I name
for both.
.PP
Each token's bits are clipped at
.I bits
(15 by default) and spread over its runes, less, in a text's first 512 tokens, a position
baseline: the bits a rune the first tokens of the tree's docs cost beyond those later in them,
since a text's start costs more for lack of context even after its preamble.
Each token's score is the clipped bits over the runes of a window of
.I width
tokens around it (32 by default), weighted by a triangle that falls off from the token.
The cutoff is the score above which the tokens cover
.I density
percent of the text's runes, from
.B $home/lib/huh
or
.B /lib/huh
as for
.IR Huh .
A stretch is a run of tokens above the cutoff for 1.3 times the density that holds a token above
the cutoff for the density, and is at least
.I tokens
tokens long (4 by default);
.B -l
.B 0
keeps every one.
A stretch's score is its tokens' scores, weighted by their runes.
With
.BR -a ,
.I huh
prints every stretch, a run of tokens above the median score, highlighted or not.
Nothing is parsed: the text is plain text to the model, and every stretch comes from the tokens'
bits and their places.
